cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) models economic and organizational activity through
  a real-resource or capability state K, actor-specific exchange-value and capital
  representations K_i, structured subjective states P_i, and actor-side action or
  process records A_i. The field is not an independent scalar value; it is a derived
  action-generating configuration of resources, capabilities, subjective states,
  capital representations, and projection-specified relations and constraints.
  Action generation is separated into feasible, cognitively or behaviorally
  available, and admissible action sets. Forecasts are represented as
  projection-selected outcome bundles, while current belief and expectation
  components of P_i support forecast generation and current preference and valuation
  components support evaluation. Multi-actor realized events are linked by shared
  event identity and constructed by reconciling participant-side records rather than
  discarding them as duplicates. Physical or resource endpoint change is represented
  through a projection-defined difference operator rather than universal subtraction.
  VFT distinguishes resource capability, realized use experience, and exchange-value
  representation. Its realized-use outcome is interval-indexed and may be called a
  VFT-specific use-value quantity when connecting to economic categories; general
  additivity and an instantaneous rate are not assumed. A rate may be introduced only
  in projections that separately define an appropriate cumulative realized-use
  process. Formal accounting is treated as a projection rather than a universal
  causal layer. Exchange-value residuals may receive projection-specific
  interpretations such as surplus, profit, income, or valuation gain. Organizations
  or companies can be modeled as actors, while a business field is the local
  action-generating configuration that reproduces recurring business activity; a
  business is the activity system centered on such a field. Entrepreneurship and new
  business development can be represented through business-oriented field formation.
  VFT retains resource-realization, activity-flow, and P-downside as constitutive
  rationality assumptions, with projection-specific admissibility, exclusion, or
  comparison rules required for their use in decision models. Marxian categories such
  as use-value, labor, exchange-value, surplus, and accumulation can be represented
  through projection-specific specializations.
